417] [17592186133822 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186367429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133822 #edb/ref 1039 "Constructing the Normal Equation Corresponding to a Linear System" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133822 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186133634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186133822 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "fda08f5d-4513-5be0-b8c4-475e82f9bf5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186134733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1045 "The Least-Squares Solution of a Linear System (With Collinearity)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186134698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134027 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "dac16d4a-58b8-5e9c-b569-e42a4c036b80" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134332 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326354 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134332 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40941" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134332 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134332 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $6a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134332 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da9cd726-dc83-58c0-961c-2d8f4017268b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134332 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 2 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 60\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 & 10 \\\\ 30 & 60 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}15 & 30 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{2}{3}-2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n6a+\\frac{b}{c}=6\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{(-2)}{1}=2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134698 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134733 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134698 #edb/ref 1061 20272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134698 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73e4890e-8287-5a80-8c3c-2c95ce05512e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134698 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186135060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134699 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134699 #edb/ref 1061 20273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134699 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2664b077-4f38-5154-af4c-d4fd21596b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134699 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134700 #edb/ref 1061 20275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134700 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4594719-c6f8-53f5-bc1a-22e17b863c14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134700 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134733 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186134699 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134733 #edb/ref 1061 22147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134733 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a43860da-1a59-5a1c-930e-23d6f893c13a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186134733 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186133822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135060 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's consider the following inconsistent system of equations:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSince the system is inconsistent, we cannot find an exact solution. However, as we've previously seen, we can attempt to find the so-called least-squares solution, which is a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that\n\n$$\n\\|  A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|\n$$\n\nis as small as possible. \n\nAs before, the least-squares solution can be found by solving the corresponding normal equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following: \n\n- Previously, we solved the normal equation by left-multiplying the normal equation by $(A^T A)^{-1}.$\n\n- However, in this case, the second column of $A$ is a multiple of the first.\n\n- So, the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent.\n\n- Therefore, $A^T A$ is not invertible.\n\nTo solve the normal equation in this case, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -20 \\\\ -20 & 80\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the last equation by Gaussian elimination, we obtain the general solution\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1+4{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that our solution is not unique! The second term of the least-squares solution (highlighted in blue) represents vectors from the null space of $A.$ \n\nIn general, if a matrix $A$ has collinear columns, then the least-squares solution to $A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b$ is not unique." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135060 #edb/ref 1065 3637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135060 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2f2e227a-264e-5e00-8c12-ea534f4b351f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186135060 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266192 #edb/ref 1080 "10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266192 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266192 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "056bde88-c84a-542b-b854-757f76eb5537" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266289 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266289 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266289 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11577138-092f-5405-8add-1bf21e092d2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266365 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266365 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266365 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a1c90f3-c1b6-573f-ab6c-07ec11bf9f28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266374 #edb/ref 1080 "-15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266374 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266374 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1af21ba1-ec9f-5759-8519-d9a10d13d566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186266376 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 