 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ -44 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\na-b+c=(-40)-(-44)+(-40)=-36.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269136 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269136 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137034" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269136 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269136 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ and vector $b$ shown above. If the general least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]\\,+\\,{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ c \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+2b+c?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269136 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef71613a-c794-54f5-9a74-68efbe48ffea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269136 #edb/ref 1074 "The least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.\n\nFrom the second row, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,⟹\\,{x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3}.\n$$\n\nFrom the first row, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\na+2b+c=1+2\\cdot 0+(-1)=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269137 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269137 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137061" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269137 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269137 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=1 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+10{x}_{2}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $2a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269137 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "26d67fbf-4de7-566c-9152-9d1736d17888" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269137 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ -5 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}30 & -60 \\\\ -60 & 120\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-15 \\\\ 30\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the above matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -2 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n2a+\\frac{b}{c}=2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{2}{1}=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269140 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186268495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269140 #edb/ref 1069 "q-137097" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269140 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269140 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2 \\\\ 5{x}_{1}-5{x}_{2}=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear equations shown above. If the general least-squares solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $-9a+\\frac{b}{c}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269140 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93179ecb-11e4-5253-ab06-7702070386da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269140 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we write our system in matrix form as $Ax=b,$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe least-squares solution of $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nNotice that the columns of $A$ are linearly dependent. So first, let's write down the normal equation in matrix form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 \\\\ -27 & 27\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system corresponding to the final matrix equation using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ -27 & 27 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}27 & -27 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we have \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n-9a+\\frac{b}{c}=-9\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{3})+\\frac{1}{1}=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323994 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323994 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323994 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "027310f9-ee0d-507a-bc28-d9da862bc3bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324150 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324150 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324150 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "13cbf72a-775b-5500-b4ce-fd7dee8abc45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324385 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324385 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324385 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "33ec9bd3-99d5-5104-8812-7b53252709d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324522 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324522 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324522 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "444cc429-dfb0-5a5b-b8d4-d4fc2403cd81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324593 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324593 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324593 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4c018f0f-7adf-5ead-8130-41b36d9554f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324776 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324776 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324776 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6058f5f8-765c-528f-b9e3-77d370e52e5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324838 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417]